# Act 1: Cast On. The format is described at the top of scripts/story.gd.
# Flow: a new game opens on act1_arrival (the rain bus) → walk to the farm gate (act1_cottage,
# Mabel and the keys) → step inside (act1_basket, Gran's basket and letter) → knit in the
# rocking chair → act1_fence (the sheep are out; meeting Rowan).

== act1_arrival
@trigger start
@once
@weather rain
@home rowan 25.6 -5.5
@home mabel 22.4 4.6
@place nora 40.2 3.4 180
@prop bus bus 42.6 0.6 0
@prop suitcase case 40.8 3.7 20
@fade out 0.01
@title Knitting Empire
@camera 41.2 2.2 10
@fade in 2.0
> Maplewood. Last stop. The rain has followed her all the way from the city.
@prop_move bus 52 0.6 5
> The bus pulls away. One suitcase, and a coat that was never meant for weather like this.
Nora (sad): Okay. You did it. You actually got on the bus.
@phone Daniel
> Her phone buzzes. She doesn't need to look. She looks anyway.
@camera 40.2 3.4 5
Nora (worried): Daniel.
? The phone keeps ringing.
- Decline the call. -> declined | phone off; set declined_daniel
- Let it ring out. -> ringout | phone off; set ignored_daniel
@label declined
> She presses the red button. It's the smallest thing she's done in months, and her hand shakes doing it.
Nora (neutral): Not today.
@goto after
@label ringout
> She watches it ring until it stops. Then a little longer, in case.
Nora (sad): Still can't do it, huh.
@label after
@prop_remove bus
@camera 40.2 3.4 8
> She hauls the suitcase up by its one good handle.
@prop_remove case
Nora (wry): Gran always said you could smell the sheep from the signpost. Gran, you were not exaggerating.
@goal Walk down the road to Birchfield Farm | 24.6 3
@set act1_arrived
@camera player
@toast Click the ground to walk. Follow the gold marker.

== act1_cottage
@trigger area farm_gate
@requires act1_arrived
@once
@goal
@walk mabel 24.0 4.2 nowait
@walk nora 25.3 3.0
@face mabel nora
@face nora mabel
@camera between mabel nora 8
Mabel (happy): There you are! I'd started to think the bus had eaten you.
Nora (surprised): Mabel? You didn't have to wait out here in this.
Mabel (warm): Course I did. Ellie would have had my hide. Here. Keys.
> The keys are old and heavy, on a loop of knitted cord gone grey with handling.
Mabel (wry): Front door sticks. Lift it a bit and shove. She never fixed it. Said it kept the salesmen out.
Nora (sad): I should have come back sooner. Before...
Mabel (neutral): You're here now. That's the bit that counts.
? What do you say?
- Thank you. For all of it. The funeral too. -> thanks | familiarity mabel +4; trust mabel +2
- Was it bad? At the end? -> theend | familiarity mabel +3; set asked_about_gran
@label thanks
Mabel (warm): Oh, hush. Half the town turned up. She'd knitted for most of them.
@goto keys
@label theend
Mabel (sad): Quiet. She was in her chair with the radio on. Needles in her lap.
Mabel (warm): I don't think she minded, love. I think she was just tired.
@label keys
Mabel (happy): The Land Office is on the plaza if you need anything. Papers, gossip, cake. Mostly cake.
@set act1_keys
@walk mabel 34 3 nowait
@goal Go inside the cottage | 9.0 3
@camera player

== act1_basket
@trigger area cottage_inside
@requires act1_keys
@once
@goal
@home mabel off
@camera 3.6 2.6 9
> Dust on everything. The clock on the mantel stopped at twenty past four.
Nora (sad): Hi, Gran.
> It still smells like her. Lanolin and woodsmoke and the lemon drops she kept in her apron.
@walk nora 2.9 2.4
@face nora 2.25 2.25
@camera 2.7 2.6 6
> Her knitting basket sits by the fire where it always did. Half a sleeve, the needles still in it.
> Tucked under the yarn is an envelope. Gran's handwriting, all loops and hurry.
Nora (surprised): "For when you come home."
? The envelope is soft from being handled.
- Open it now. -> open | set read_gran_letter
- Not yet. Keep it for later. -> later | set kept_gran_letter
@label open
> "Nora, love. If you're reading this, I've gone and done the one thing I always told you not to: left without saying goodbye. Sorry about that."
> "The flock knows what to do even when you don't. The yarn in the basket is the good stuff. Don't you dare save it for best."
> "And whatever sent you home, it doesn't get to follow you through that door. Knit something. It helps. It always helped. Gran."
Nora (sad): ...Okay. Okay, Gran.
@goto knit
@label later
Nora (sad): Not yet. I can't yet.
> She slips the envelope into her coat pocket, over her heart, where it sits heavy.
@label knit
@give wool 3
@toast Gran's good yarn: +3 wool
Nora (warm): One row. Just to see if my hands remember.
@goal Sit in the rocking chair and knit | 2.4 3.6
@set act1_basket
@camera player

== act1_fence
@trigger event knit_done
@requires act1_basket
@once
@goal
> Somewhere outside, very close, something says "baa". Then something else says it louder.
Nora (surprised): That is... not far enough away.
@fade out 0.6
@weather clear
@sheep out
@prop rail rail1 18.6 2.3 0
@home rowan 16.3 2.7
@place nora 9.4 3.2 90
@face rowan nora
@camera 13.6 2.2 11
@fade in 0.8
> The rain has stopped. Three of Gran's sheep are standing in the vegetable beds like they pay rent. A board hangs off the fence.
> And there's a man with a shepherd's crook watching them, like he's been there a while.
@walk nora 14.9 4.1
@face nora rowan
@face rowan nora
@camera between rowan nora 6.5
Rowan (wry): Afternoon. Yours, I think.
Nora (worried): Are they? I mean, yes. Probably. How can you tell?
Rowan (neutral): Ellie's blue mark on the ear. And that one's eating your lettuce.
Nora (wry): I don't have lettuce.
Rowan (wry): Not anymore.
Rowan (neutral): Rowan. I keep the flock up on the hill. Ellie and I traded favours. Mostly she fed me and I fixed things.
Nora (neutral): Nora. The granddaughter. The one from the city.
Rowan (warm): I know. She talked about you. A lot, actually.
Rowan (neutral): Right. I'll get the gate open, you stand there and look big.
@walk rowan 16.3 -0.2
@anim rowan Work
@camera between rowan nora 9.5
@wait 0.6
@shake 0.5
> The gate slams back in the wind, a crack like a gunshot.
Nora (surprised): Ah!
> Her whole body jerks. Her hands are up before she can stop them.
@anim rowan off
@face rowan nora
@face nora rowan
Rowan (worried): Hey. You alright?
? Rowan watches her, careful, the way you'd watch a skittish ewe.
- I'm fine. It just made me jump. -> fine | familiarity rowan +2; set nora_deflected
- Sorry. Loud noises. Long story. -> honest | trust rowan +3; familiarity rowan +2; set nora_opened
@label fine
Rowan (neutral): Right. Course.
> He doesn't push. He does wedge the gate with a stone so it can't do that again.
@goto roundup
@label honest
Rowan (warm): Nothing to be sorry for. I'll keep the gate quiet.
> He wedges it with a stone, gently, like the gate's the one that needs calming down.
Nora (warm): Thanks.
@label roundup
Rowan (happy): Arms out, walk slow, and whatever you do, don't run. They can smell fear and they think it's funny.
@fade out 0.8
> Twenty minutes of slow walking, outstretched arms, and one sheep that would not be told.
@sheep home
@prop_remove rail1
@place nora 14.6 3.8 90
@home rowan 15.8 2.6
@face rowan nora
@face nora rowan
@camera between rowan nora 5.5
@fade in 0.8
Rowan (wry): Not bad. You only fell over once.
Nora (happy): Twice. You missed the second one.
Rowan (happy): I didn't.
> It's the first time she's laughed in a long while. It comes out rusty.
Rowan (neutral): I'll come by tomorrow and fix that fence properly. I'll bring nails. You bring tea.
? What do you say?
- Deal. I make terrible tea. -> deal | familiarity rowan +3
- You don't have to do that. -> nohelp | trust rowan +1; familiarity rowan +1
@label deal
Rowan (wry): Good. Ellie's was terrible too. I'd hate to get used to anything else.
@goto bye
@label nohelp
Rowan (warm): I know. That's sort of the point.
@label bye
@set met_rowan
@set act1_done
> He tips the crook at her like a hat and heads off up the hill, whistling at nothing.
@walk rowan 24 4.4 nowait
@wait 2.5
@fade out 1.2
@home rowan off
@title Act 1: Cast On
@title to be continued
@fade in 1.2
@toast Act 1 complete. The farm is yours now.
